

📊 2. Introduce “Composite Quality Scores”

Right now, each flag is binary — it’s either raised or not.
You could aggregate a score-based system that blends the signals into a quality tier:

# Compute overall quality score (0–100)
quality_score = 100
for flag in report.all_flags:
    if flag.severity == RedFlagSeverity.CRITICAL:
        quality_score -= 15
    elif flag.severity == RedFlagSeverity.WARNING:
        quality_score -= 7
    elif flag.severity == RedFlagSeverity.INFO:
        quality_score -= 2

quality_score = max(0, quality_score)
report.quality_score = quality_score


Then classify:

80–100: Excellent

60–80: Acceptable

40–60: Risky

<40: Red flag zone

This gives a quantitative feel for the company’s financial health and makes backtesting easier.

💰 3. Add “Cash Conversion Cycle” Analysis

Peter Lynch often looked for companies with efficient operations and quick inventory turns.
You can use:

cash_conversion_cycle = days_inventory_outstanding + days_sales_outstanding - days_payables_outstanding


and flag if it’s rising sharply or very high relative to peers (if data available).

🧾 4. Add “Earnings Manipulation Detection” (Beneish M-Score Lite)

This is a big one: you can detect potential accounting trickery by combining ratios like:

Days Sales Receivable Index (DSRI)

Gross Margin Index (GMI)

Asset Quality Index (AQI)

Sales Growth Index (SGI)

Depreciation Index (DEPI)

Accruals

Even if you don’t compute the full M-Score, add lightweight variants:

if dsri > 1.2 or gmi > 1.1:
    flags.append(RedFlag(
        category="Profitability",
        severity=RedFlagSeverity.WARNING,
        title="Possible Earnings Manipulation",
        description="Unusual revenue or margin patterns suggesting earnings management"
    ))


This will catch aggressive accounting behavior before it shows up in OCF/NI divergence.

🔄 5. Smooth Volatile Metrics (Rolling Averages)

Some ratios are too noisy quarter-to-quarter.
Replace raw comparisons with rolling 3–4 quarter averages:

avg_ocf = np.mean(ocf.values[-4:])
avg_net_income = np.mean(net_income.values[-4:])


Same for margins and leverage — this filters out seasonal or one-time effects (especially for cyclicals).

⚙️ 6. Add “Capital Efficiency” Metrics (Lynch’s Favorites)

Lynch’s high-quality companies (like Dunkin’ or Apple) show:

High ROIC

Consistent ROE

Stable or rising margins

You already check ROE and margin trends — extend it with:

roic = nopat / (total_debt + equity - cash)
if roic < 0.05:
    flags.append(RedFlag(...))


Then combine ROE + ROIC for a “capital efficiency” subscore.

🔔 7. Detect “Overvaluation + Weak Fundamentals” Combo

You already detect P/E, P/B, PEG, etc.
The next step: only raise valuation flags if fundamentals are also weak.
Example:

if pe_ratio > 50 and roe < 10:
    flags.append(RedFlag(
        category="Valuation",
        severity=RedFlagSeverity.WARNING,
        title="High Valuation Without Supporting Profitability",
        description="Expensive and low quality — potential bubble valuation"
    ))


This reduces false positives for fast-growing quality firms.

🧩 8. Add “Trend Reversal” Logic

Sometimes metrics deteriorate, but not catastrophically — e.g., margins dropping for 3 periods.
Add trend detection:

if all(m < n for m, n in zip(operating_margin[-3:], operating_margin[-2:])):
    flags.append(...)


This detects early warning signs, not just hard thresholds.

💬 9. Improve Flag Explanations

You can make descriptions adaptive to add real interpretability:

if ocf_ni_ratio < 0.8 and fcf_ni_ratio >= 0.7:
    cause = "Likely due to high reinvestment (CapEx-heavy period)"
else:
    cause = "Likely poor earnings quality"


Then include:

description=f"Earnings quality concern - {cause}."


→ It reads like a financial analyst note, not a static rule hit.

🧱 10. Add Structural Metadata for Backtesting & Visualization

Each flag could carry:

@dataclass
class RedFlag:
    ...
    score_impact: float = 0.0  # + for positive signal, - for risk
    metric: Optional[str] = None  # e.g. "debt_to_equity"


That lets you:

Track which metrics most often trigger red flags

Weight them in backtesting

Visualize your “Red Flag Radar” for each company.

🚀 TL;DR: Top 5 Practical Enhancements
Type	Change	Benefit
1	Industry-aware thresholds	Reduce false positives
2	Quality scoring system	Quantitative overview
3	Add ROIC & accrual metrics	True quality screening
4	Combine valuation + fundamentals	Avoid punishing strong growth stocks
5	Add M-score-like manipulation checks	Detect early fraud/risk



